INT. TAXIDERMY SHOP - BACK ROOM - NIGHT

A cramped workroom lit by one hanging bulb. Glass eyes in trays, a half-skinned fox on the table, a dead radiator ticking.

ODALYS REYES (50s, heavy apron, reading glasses on a cord) sews the fox's belly. Her son BRAM (20s, thin, jittery, car keys in his fist) stands in the doorway. PIP (15, braids, school hoodie) sits on a stool sorting glass eyes. OLD HOSEA (80s, wool cap, cane) waits with a shoebox on his knees.

BRAM
Ma, the bank called again.

ODALYS
Not in front of customers.

BRAM
He's not a customer, he's Hosea. He's been sitting there an hour.

HOSEA
Forty minutes. I'm timing it.

ODALYS
Bram, put the keys down.

BRAM
Where's the money, Ma? Where's the deposit from the Hendry job?

Odalys stops sewing. Pip stops sorting.

PIP
Uncle Bram, don't.

BRAM
Stay out of it, Pipsqueak.

ODALYS
I paid the freezer man. I paid your tuition. You want to see the receipts, you ask me nicely.

BRAM
I'm asking!

He slams the keys on the table. The fox jumps. Glass eyes scatter across the floor. Pip dives after them. Hosea lifts his cane in front of his shoebox like a shield.

ODALYS
Look what you did.

Odalys and Bram both go for the same falling tray. Their hands collide. Both freeze, nose to nose.

BRAM
(quietly)
I lost the Hendry money, Ma. At the track. All of it.

Silence. The radiator ticks. Pip, on her knees, holds up a single blue glass eye.

PIP
Found one.

Odalys lets out a long breath. She takes the eye from Pip and sets it in the fox's socket.

ODALYS
Pick up the rest, Bram. Every one.

Bram kneels. Hosea sets his shoebox on the table and lifts the lid. Inside, a small stiff canary.

HOSEA
Whenever you two are done. Her name was Dolores.

ODALYS
(gently)
Bring her here, Hosea.
